---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="kg-to-stone">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="kg-to-stone" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The decimal-stone trap</h2>
    <p>
      People get kg to stone wrong more than any other conversion, and always in the same way. A
      converter says 75 kg is <strong>11.81 stone</strong>, and the reader hears "eleven stone eight".
      That is wrong. The 0.81 is a fraction of fourteen pounds, so it is 11.34 pounds: 75 kg is
      <strong>11 st 11.3 lb</strong>, nearly four pounds more than the misreading.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this page shows stone and pounds as the main answer, and decimal stone as secondary.
    </p>

    <h2>How the numbers relate</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1 stone = 14 pounds, exactly</li>
      <li>1 stone = 6.35029318 kg (14 &times; 0.45359237)</li>
      <li>1 kg = 0.157473 stone</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So kilograms to stone is a division by 6.35029318. The whole part is the stone, and the remainder
      multiplied by fourteen is the pounds.
    </p>

    <h2>Common body weights</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kilograms</th><th scope="col">Stone and pounds</th><th scope="col">Pounds</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>50</td><td>7 st 12.2 lb</td><td>110.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>55</td><td>8 st 9.3 lb</td><td>121.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>60</td><td>9 st 6.3 lb</td><td>132.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>65</td><td>10 st 3.3 lb</td><td>143.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>70</td><td>11 st 0.3 lb</td><td>154.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>75</td><td>11 st 11.3 lb</td><td>165.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80</td><td>12 st 8.4 lb</td><td>176.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>90</td><td>14 st 2.4 lb</td><td>198.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>15 st 10.5 lb</td><td>220.5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why Britain still uses stone</h2>
    <p>
      The UK went metric for trade in the 1970s and 1980s, but not for body weight. Scales, gyms and
      conversation still use stone and pounds, but GP records, prescriptions and official forms use
      kilograms. So most British adults know their weight in a unit their doctor does not use. Ireland
      is the same. In all other countries, including the United States, the stone went out of general
      use over a century ago.
    </p>

    <h2>Historic stones were not all fourteen pounds</h2>
    <p>
      Before standardisation the stone varied by trade: eight pounds for meat, fourteen for wool,
      sixteen for cheese, twenty-four for some produce. The 14 lb stone survived because it was the
      wool stone, and wool was the most important trade. It is the only one still in use, and this
      page uses it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
